{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793875445,"nonce":"6sJbgK8a1LPUOWhbIdr8ZWeroFM=","title":"Skip redundant drop analysis for collector-owned runtime storage","message":"Lua and Python enable both automatic release and MarkSweep. The HIR preparation audit (1be184c) measured drop insertion at about 0.91 ms of 8.51 ms of timed optimization for method_call; this is diagnostic timing, not a predicted wall-time improvement.\n\nIntroduce an opt-in runtime policy that defers plain pool storage and strings to the collector while preserving dynamic-box droppers, container cleanup, type release glue, foreign releases, and explicit frees. Keep ownership summaries complete. The automatic_release boolean is not a pass-disable switch.\n\nFirst implementation should apply to lazy interpreter preparation and tier-up through their shared cached facts. Snapshot construction remains on the existing early-release policy; precompiled snapshot cleanup is retained. Keep Lua/Python defaults unchanged until validation and timing establish the tradeoff.\n\nValidate plain and phi-carried allocations, foreign and box cleanup, interpreter and native execution under GC pressure, cache/snapshot boundaries, and full language conformance including the complete pinned official TypeScript regression baseline before committing shared compiler changes. Measure frozen release binaries before heavy corpus on the benchmark host; record collection work and memory as well as compilation latency. Do not equate disabling releases with preserving arbitrary host destructors through a raw memory sweep.","files":null}]}